1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. d4 { The Scotch Game had always remained in the shadow of the Ruy Lopez and the Italian Game, until Garry Kasparov started playing it successfully in the 1990s. Since then, many players have decided to follow the world champion's example and nowadays it is a popular opening on both professional and amateur level. } exd4 4. Nxd4 Nf6 { The main and most reliable move. } 5. Nxc6 bxc6 6. e5 { -KEY- } Qe7 { Black blocks his bishop, but forces White to do the same. } 7. Qe2 Nd5 8. g3 { An old move which is considered to be dodgy nowadays. } g6 { A typical way to develop the bishop. } 9. c4 Ba6 { Usually after 9.c4 Black has two options ...Ba6 and ... Nb6. Usually both moves are fine but in this repertoire we favour ...Nb6 move. This is the only exception as we have a chance to put enormous pressure with a rapid development (...Bg7, ...0-0-0, ... Rhe8). } 10. Bg2 { Not playing b3 leads to problems with the c4-pawn: } Bg7 11. O-O O-O 12. Nd2 { This was suggested by Milos Pavlovic in his book 'The Modernized Scotch Game' (Thinkers Publishing 2019) but Black is more than fine here. } Nb6 13. f4 Qc5+ 14. Rf2 Nxc4 $15 { The position is complex but Black is a pawn up. } 
